{"title":"Incenter/excenter lemma","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File/Thumb\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Incenter_excenter_lemma.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/e/e2/Incenter_excenter_lemma.png/200px-Incenter_excenter_lemma.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"200\" height=\"286\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/e/e2/Incenter_excenter_lemma.png 1.5x\" /></a><figcaption>Diagram of the configuration.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>In <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Geometry\" title=\"Geometry\">geometry</a>, the <b>incenter/excenter lemma</b>, sometimes called the <b>Trillium theorem</b>, <b>Chicken Foot lemma</b>, and <b>Fact 5</b>, is a result concerning a relationship between the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Incenter\" title=\"Incenter\">incenter</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Excenter\" title=\"Excenter\">excenter</a> of a triangle. Given any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> with incenter <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />-excenter <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/2/822b800ca9993bd5686e4fe3a128c4659d3e979d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I_A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" />, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/9/859ccf4cd60c7bc6b8fa1afc9a42dc811a826d6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> be the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/9/f997f2e439fbb9578ac4d715bc866bbea93ebc2a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overarc{BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"23\" height=\"20\" /> on the triangle's circumcenter. Then, the theorem states that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/9/859ccf4cd60c7bc6b8fa1afc9a42dc811a826d6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> is the center of a circle through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/2/822b800ca9993bd5686e4fe3a128c4659d3e979d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I_A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>The incenter/excenter lemma makes frequent appearances in olympiad geometry. Along with the larger lemma, two smaller results follow: first, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/9/859ccf4cd60c7bc6b8fa1afc9a42dc811a826d6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/2/822b800ca9993bd5686e4fe3a128c4659d3e979d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I_A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" /> are collinear, and second, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/2/822b800ca9993bd5686e4fe3a128c4659d3e979d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I_A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" /> is the reflection of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> across <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/9/859ccf4cd60c7bc6b8fa1afc9a42dc811a826d6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />. Both of these follow easily from the main proof.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof\">Proof</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Incenter/excenter_lemma&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Proof\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/d/2cd146d6f7a60e1e05e30f0cfd58eff8b184d607.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A = \\angle BAC$\" width=\"93\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/d/b5d78933af7e4fef8ae59b4eee32d19b34c19970.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B = \\angle CBA$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"96\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/e/6de586db9f451779f29d65aae41a24f69953348d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C = \\angle ACB$\" width=\"93\" height=\"13\" />, and note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/9/859ccf4cd60c7bc6b8fa1afc9a42dc811a826d6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> are collinear (as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/9/859ccf4cd60c7bc6b8fa1afc9a42dc811a826d6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> is on the angle bisector). We are going to show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/c/8fc5b32fae29d3d2f5d0add9b4eaaccc756b425a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$LB = LI$\" width=\"73\" height=\"12\" />, the other cases being similar.\nFirst, notice that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/0/ef044ac8f791a66dc6a123cf797af6be15dbb36f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle LBI = \\angle LBC + \\angle CBI = \\angle LAC + \\angle CBI = \\angle IAC + \\angle CBI = \\frac{1}{2} A + \\frac{1}{2} B.\\]\" width=\"571\" height=\"37\" /> However, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/d/6fd54602cd51ee1807548259c2479e05d52fd7b4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle BIL = \\angle BAI + \\angle ABI = \\frac{1}{2} A + \\frac{1}{2} B.\\]\" width=\"301\" height=\"37\" /> Hence, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/6/9e6eae5fcf4e6079ab54ff15520664ae82406bb4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle BIL$\" width=\"52\" height=\"13\" /> is isosceles, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/c/8fc5b32fae29d3d2f5d0add9b4eaaccc756b425a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$LB = LI$\" width=\"73\" height=\"12\" />. The rest of the proof proceeds along these lines. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6cf65b1f2fbdf388e3daeff9b96b34d3399d777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\square$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Incenter/excenter_lemma&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Orthic_triangle\" title=\"Orthic triangle\">Orthic triangle</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Geometry/Olympiad\" title=\"Geometry/Olympiad\"> Olympiad geometry</a></li></ul>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Incenter/excenter_lemma&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017171459\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.021 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.035 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 170/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 310/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 5410/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.292      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.292      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:21242:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017171459 and revision id 262952. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}